<!--
  Skeleton for a project rule. Copy to <project>/.claude/rules/<topic>.md and fill in.

  Do NOT keep this file inside .claude/rules/ — every .md there is discovered
  recursively, so an unfilled example would load as a real rule.

  A rule is the guarantee, a skill is the explanation. Rules load unconditionally
  for every file matching `paths`, so they carry the "always / never" list and link
  out for the reasoning. Target 25 lines; if it grows past that, what you are
  writing is a skill.

  One file per topic or per app. Name it after the topic: reports.md, users-auth.md.

  Belongs here: invariants of this app that fail silently, contracts between two
  modules, the reason a surprising decision was made deliberately.

  Does not: anything a reader can see in the code, anything the generic rules or a
  skill already cover, step-by-step procedures.
-->

---
paths:
  - "**/apps/<app>/**"
---

# <Topic>

<!-- One or two sentences: what this app owns, and the single invariant that holds
     across it. If a newcomer breaks one thing here, this is the thing. -->

<!-- Then the rules. Lead each with the imperative in bold, then say what breaks
     without it — a rule whose consequence is unstated gets treated as a preference.

**Never <do X> by hand.** <What it drifts from, and why the derived value is right.>

**<Contract between two modules>.** <What must change in the same commit, and what
silently rots if it doesn't.>
-->

<!-- A table when the rule is a mapping — status to allowed operations, override to
     effect. Prose loses these.

| <Status> | Allowed |
|---|---|
| `<value>` | <what may happen in this state> |
-->

<!-- A blockquote for the trap that returns success while doing nothing. These are
     the entries that justify the rule existing at all.

> ⚠️ <The call that looks correct and silently does nothing.>
-->

